{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the frog came up.</b> It was one frog, and they would strike it and it would spew out swarm after swarm — this is its midrash (Sanhedrin 67). And its plain sense is to say that the swarm of frogs is called in singular form; and so ״וַתְּהִי הַכִּנָּם״ — the swarming, פדולייר\"א בְּלַעַ\"ז, and likewise <b>and the frog came up</b> — גרינולייר\"א בְּלַעַ\"ז."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "The two la'azim appear to be Old French words meaning 'brood/swarm' (cf. modern French pousser, grainer); kept in Hebrew script per protocol."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:51:02.821030084Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}